The strongest reason your reading is wrong is that ?�the helper VI?�s `Run` returned successfully??does **not** prove that `Generic.Delete` executed without a LabVIEW error. `Generic.Delete` is an Invoke Node with its own `error in/error out`; if `error in.status` is already true, LabVIEW does not execute the method and merely propagates the error. That internal error does not become an ActiveX exception merely because the helper was launched through `Run VI`. [NI Invoke Node reference](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-US/bundle/labview-api-ref/page/functions/invoke-node.html), [NI standard error-in behavior](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-US/bundle/labview/page/using-the-standard-functionality-for-error-in-parameters.html)

Thus the leading alternative is: `Traverse`, `Index Array`/casting, or an earlier node produced an error?�or `Delete` itself returned an edit-state error?�but `OpDelete_v0` did not expose that final error cluster to COM. That exactly predicts ?�Run succeeded, zero objects disappeared, saved file unchanged??without any silent-delete behavior.

The observation that falsifies your claim is either:

- `Delete.error out` is nonzero, proving the failure was not silent; or
- immediately after `Delete.error out.status = FALSE`, reading `UID` through the **same original GObject reference** fails because the reference is invalid. NI explicitly says all references become invalid after successful deletion. [NI Generic Delete](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-US/bundle/labview-api-ref/page/properties-and-methods/vi-server/generic/delete.html)

### 1. When does `Generic.Delete` return no error yet delete nothing?

I found **no documented such condition**. NI?�s current contract simply says that it deletes the object and invalidates all references; it also says the method is unavailable while the owning VI is running. It documents no Property-node exception and no ?�silently ignore if noneditable??behavior. [NI Generic Delete](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-US/bundle/labview-api-ref/page/properties-and-methods/vi-server/generic/delete.html)

Concrete conditions that can produce the *appearance* of a zero-error no-op are:

- **A pre-existing error reaches `error in`.** The Invoke Node is skipped and propagates the existing error. If the helper does not return that cluster, COM sees only a successful helper execution. [NI standard error-in behavior](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-US/bundle/labview/page/using-the-standard-functionality-for-error-in-parameters.html)
- **`Delete` generates an internal error that is never retrieved.** Wiring an error output disables reliance on automatic error dialogs; LabVIEW errors are data that must be inspected or handled. [NI error-handling documentation](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-AS/bundle/labview-api-ref/page/dialog-boxes/execution-page-vi-properties-dialog-box.html)
- **The individual object is locked.** NI says locked objects cannot be deleted, moved, or have properties modified. I found no NI documentation establishing whether scripted `Delete` reports an error or silently refuses this particular case, so this remains a plausible but unverified alternative. [NI object-lock behavior](https://knowledge.ni.com/KnowledgeArticleDetails?id=kA00Z0000019SMvSAM)
- **Verification is observing another in-memory target/application instance.** Multiple application instances have separate VI Server contexts, so this remains possible unless the delete and verification share the same VI and GObject references. [NI VI Server configuration](https://www.ni.com/docs/ar-SD/csh?context=lvcore_lvdialog_vi_server_config_options)

Running/reserved state is not documented as silent: NI says editing a running or reserved VI raises error 1000, and a strictly typed VI reference reserves the VI until that reference is closed. [NI error-1000 explanation](https://knowledge.ni.com/KnowledgeArticleDetails?id=kA00Z000000P8c9SAC)

### 2. Property indicating editability

There is no documented single `Editable?` property.

The minimum public checks are:

- `Execution:State == Idle` (`1`). Idle means the VI is in memory and not running; `Running` also covers reservation by an active hierarchy. [NI Execution State](https://knowledge.ni.com/KnowledgeArticleDetails?id=kA03q000000YIEJCA4)
- `Lock State:Get == Not Locked` (`1`). The documented enumeration is `0 Invalid`, `1 Not Locked`, `2 Locked`, `3 Password-protected`. [Lock State:Get reference](https://documentation.help/NI-LabView-Local-Project-Settings/VI_Get_Lock_State.html)
- If applicable, also check the owning library/class lock. Locked libraries can prevent editing of their contents in some contexts. [NI locked-library example](https://knowledge.ni.com/KnowledgeArticleDetails?id=kA00Z000000kFKSSA2), [NI class locking across application instances](https://knowledge.ni.com/KnowledgeArticleDetails?id=kA00Z000000P704SAC)

`Edit Mode On Open` is not an editability test; it is a saved window-opening preference. A VI can also be manually placed in run mode without executing, so `Idle` is necessary but not a complete editability guarantee. [NI run/edit-mode explanation](https://knowledge.ni.com/KnowledgeArticleDetails?id=kA00Z000000kKQuSAM)

Your measured `Execution:State == 1` strongly attacks H1 and the running/reserved part of H3?�provided it was read from the same in-memory VI instance that owns UID 1319.

### 3. Attached wires

Attached wires are not a documented reason to refuse deletion. `Generic.Delete` has no ?�must be unwired??precondition. Deleting a node may remove the relevant connection/branch and leave other required terminals unwired or loose/broken wiring, making the VI nonexecutable; LabVIEW explicitly supports temporarily broken diagrams while editing. [NI Generic Delete](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-US/bundle/labview-api-ref/page/properties-and-methods/vi-server/generic/delete.html), [NI wiring and broken-wire behavior](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-AS/bundle/labview/page/using-wires-to-link-block-diagram-objects.html)

Therefore wires predict ?�object deleted, diagram potentially broken,??not ?�zero objects deleted.??
